  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Massion's 2024 article on terminology theory in the age of artificial intelligence has accumulated 6 citations within two years of publication, with downstream reuse spanning translation studies, applied linguistics, healthcare, and computational language work across four countries. Early evidence suggests the work is catalyzing methodological discussion at the intersection of human language practice and machine learning systems.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in the Journal of Translation Studies, \"Terminology in the Age of AI\" examines how advances in generative AI and large language models fundamentally challenge and reshape terminology theory and practice. The article's central argument concerns the interaction between embodied human language and the algorithmic, mathematical language of AI systems\u2014a distinction that has implications for how terminology professionals, translators, and language technologists understand their craft in an era of machine learning. The work's framing of terminology as central to accurate multilingual communication positions it at a critical juncture in applied linguistics and language technology.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape reveals disciplinary uptake concentrated in translation studies and institutional terminology work. Tanja Wissik, affiliated with the Austrian Academy of Sciences, has cited the article twice\u2014once in a 2025 article on generative AI adoption by terminology professionals in institutional settings (published in Tradum\u00e0tica tecnologies de la traducci\u00f3) and again in a 2026 book chapter on AI's impact on institutional terminology work. Ralph Kr\u00fcger's 2025 article in Lebende Sprachen examines generative AI implementation from a competence perspective, indicating that Massion's framing has influenced how practitioners conceptualize workforce adaptation to AI tools. A 2026 article in the Annals of The Royal College of Surgeons of England applied the work's insights to patient education materials generated by large language models, suggesting reach beyond translation studies into healthcare communication.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The geographic and sectoral diversity of citing works\u2014spanning Austria, Germany, the United Kingdom, and China, with representation from education, healthcare, and government sectors\u2014indicates that the article's core question resonates across different language industry contexts. The work types citing it include peer-reviewed journal articles, book chapters, and conference presentations, suggesting integration into both research discourse and professional practice discussions. While six citations represents an early signal rather than established broad impact, the pattern shows engagement from researchers actively shaping how terminology and translation practices are transforming in response to AI.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory"
